<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Mentana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Mentana was fought on 3 November 1867 near Mentana, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, between the volunteers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>, who were trying to seize Rome, and papal troops supported by a French expeditionary force. The French and papal victory, aided by the new French Chassepot rifle, kept Rome under the pope until the French garrison was withdrawn in 1870.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mentana.jpg" alt="Battle of Mentana" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lionel Royer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lionel-No%C3%ABl_Royer_-_The_Battle_Near_Mentana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was declared capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> on 27 March 1861, but a French garrison kept there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a>. Garibaldi's first attempt on the city, under the slogan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a> o Morte, was stopped by Italian troops at Aspromonte in 1862. By the September Convention of 15 September 1864 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> undertook to protect the Papal States and moved its capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was to withdraw its garrison within two years. After Italy gained Mantua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a> in 1866, the last French battalions left Civitavecchia that December.</p><p>At a congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> on 9 September 1867 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, and he was left free to raise about 10,000 volunteers to march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> while a rising broke out inside it. His open preparations gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> time to send a relief force.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In October 1867 the volunteers invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>. On 22 October revolutionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> and Piazza Colonna, and a mine exploded under the Serristori barracks of the Papal Zouaves, killing 27 people, but the rising was put down. Enrico Cairoli was mortally wounded at Villa Glori and his brother Giovanni severely wounded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>, with about 8,100 men, took Monterotondo and waited for an insurrection that never came, while a French force landed at Civitavecchia.</p><p>Early on 3 November papal troops under General Hermann Kanzler and French troops under General Balthazar de Polhès advanced along the Via Nomentana. The volunteers had almost no artillery or cavalry. Driven from a position south of Mentana, they held the fortified village against repeated attacks until three companies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zouaves">Zouaves</a> seized the road to Monterotondo. Garibaldi could not stop the rout; survivors held out in the castle, and some surrendered the next morning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 4 November Garibaldi withdrew to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> with 5,100 men. The Ara dei Caduti at Mentana stands over the mass grave of his fallen. A French garrison stayed at Civitavecchia until August 1870, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> recalled it, and the Italian Army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 20 September 1870.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Mentana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century the small papal army, reinforced by foreign volunteers including the papal Zouaves, was defeated by Piedmont at Castelfidardo in 1860 and held Rome only with French help, winning at <b>Mentana</b> in 1867.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li></ul></section>
